<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eóganacht Chaisil</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Eóganacht of Cashel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganacht</a> Chaisil was the leading branch of the Eóganachta, the dynasty claiming descent from the legendary Eógan Mór that dominated the kingship of Munster from about the 5th century to the 10th. Its kings ruled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rock-of-cashel">Rock of Cashel</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-tipperary">County Tipperary</a>. Older peoples such as the Corcu Loígde and the Déisi Muman became subject kingdoms paying them tribute, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-cais">Dál gCais</a> displaced them in the 10th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eoganacht-chaisil.jpg" alt="Eóganacht Chaisil" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Barry (1741 — 1806) of Cork · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Barry%27s_The_Baptism_of_King_Cashel_by_St_Patrick.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
